{"packet_id": "f832c1635cbb55c2", "dimensions": {"problem_framing": {"status": "scored", "score": 2, "event_ids": ["A1"], "reason": "A1 separates the objective from the suggested solution and lists real alternatives: a differentiated proposition, a non-differentiated extension, no expansion, and licensing. It reaches rung 2 but not 3. It assumes a marine recreational use case without noting that the PLB excerpt (acr-fr-purpose) describes general-public use in a remote environment as a last resort. The €20,000 / 12-week budget is not in the dossier. It is treated here as a protocol assumption, not an observed fact."}, "causal_mechanism": {"status": "scored", "score": 1, "event_ids": ["A9"], "reason": "A9 names who benefits and how value is captured through premium direct sales. The behavioural driver is asserted without support: \"Recreational mariners currently lack affordable, reliable emergency communication tools.\" The phrase \"device that can be used by friends and family\" is not explained. No dossier source supports the claimed gap. In effect this names a benefit rather than tracing a supported chain."}, "distinct_theories": {"status": "scored", "score": 0, "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"], "reason": "A10 (T2) is nearly a verbatim copy of A9 (T1). It only appends \"and they trust ACR's reputation,\" which is already one of T1's listed assumptions. Same customer, product, channel and value capture. This is one theory, or copies."}, "assumptions_predictions": {"status": "scored", "score": 2, "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"], "reason": "The predictions are observable: survey purchase intent above $100, and regional sales correlating with boating activity. They are weakly threatening. \"Market share ... measurable within 12 months\" would hold almost regardless of the theory. The $100 threshold is a proposed assumption, not an observation. The assumptions do not say which failure would sink the theory."}, "evidence_judgement": {"status": "scored", "score": 1, "event_ids": ["